<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hugh Capet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 940–<a href="/years/996">996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hugh Capet was Duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and, from 987, King of the Franks, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a>. Son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>, he held lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and was elected king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a> after the death of the last Carolingian king, Louis V. His royal power was limited, but by crowning his son Robert as co-king he secured a dynasty whose branches ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> until 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hugh-capet.jpg" alt="Hugh Capet" width="227" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LeThéodorus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SceauHuguesCapet_(cropped).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hugh was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>, Duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, and Hedwige of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, daughter of the German king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>. Through his mother he was a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>, and through his paternal grandmother he descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. His family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robertians">Robertians</a>, were powerful landowners in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a>, and two of them, Odo I and Robert I, had been chosen as kings in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>. When his father died in 956 Hugh, the eldest son, was about fifteen, and his neighbours took advantage: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-i-of-blois">Theobald I of Blois</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> and Châteaudun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 960 King Lothair agreed that Hugh should have his father's margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> and the title Duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. In 978, when Otto II invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and besieged Lothair in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, a relief army under Hugh helped force him to lift the siege on 30 November, and Otto's rearguard was destroyed crossing the flooded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a>. The victory restored Hugh's position as the first noble of the kingdom.</p><p>Lothair's son Louis V died in a riding accident while hunting in the forest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a> on 22 May 987, leaving no children. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-duke-of-lower-lorraine">Charles, Duke of Lower Lorraine</a>, a Carolingian claimant, was accused of having made himself a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the emperor. Adalberon, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>, argued that the throne was not acquired by hereditary right. Hugh was crowned rex Francorum at Noyon on 1 June and again in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 3 July 987, and he had his son Robert crowned on 25 December that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-duke-of-lower-lorraine">Charles of Lorraine</a> took Laon, and Hugh and Robert twice besieged it without success. Adalberon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Laon, then seized Charles in his sleep and delivered him to Hugh, who imprisoned him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> until his death. Hugh's power was narrow. He held towns and estates between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Orléans of about 400 square miles, and a plot of 993 to hand him to Otto III went unpunished, which shows how tenuous his hold was. He had to seek military aid from Richard I of Normandy, though his unanimous election gave him moral authority.</p><p>Hugh died on 24 October 996 and was buried at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-denis">Basilica of Saint-Denis</a>. His son Robert continued to reign, and the direct Capetians ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 987 to 1328, with cadet branches after that until 1848.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hugh Capet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Rudolph became King of West Francia in 923, and the duchy later passed through several hands until Henry the Great, brother of <b>Hugh Capet</b>, ruled it from 965 to 1002.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the last Carolingian king, Louis V, died in a hunting accident in 987, the great nobles and bishops, led by Archbishop Adalbero of Reims, elected <b>Hugh Capet</b>, duke of the Franks, and he was crowned in July 987.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Frankish kingdom was at least partly elective: Pepin the Short was elected king of the Franks in 751 with papal approval, and the West Frankish nobles chose between rival lineages until they elected <b>Hugh Capet</b> in 987.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hugh Capet</b> was crowned, replacing the Carolingian line with his own family.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-3">3 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His grandson <b>Hugh Capet</b> later founded the Capetian dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-15">15 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He died a year later without an heir, and the nobles elected <b>Hugh Capet</b>, founder of the Capetian dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dourdan, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Les Juifs, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.53,2.01&amp;year=976" aria-label="Dourdan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hugh Capet"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5114 5973h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5114 5973h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.53,2.01&amp;year=976">What was here in 976 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
